2.3 Dcentralized FL
Decentralized FL [86, 106, 115, 158, 209], a novel server-less paradigm of FL, emphasizes the advantages of employing a peer-to-peer model delivery and aggregation network that is free from the dependencies of a central trusted server. Instead of solely communicating with a central server, participants can fully leverage the network bandwidth by utilizing the network connections between them. For example, Lalitha et al. [115] proposed exchange and merge of posterior distribution among neighboring users to collaboratively estimate the global optimal parameter. Similar to the local training of FedAvg, DFedAvgM [209] also suggests that each client communicates with its neighbors after multiple training iterations to improve the convergence rate of training. On the other hand, ProxyFL [106] improves the privacy of neighbor-wide model sharing by sharing a proxy model through knowledge distillation [85]. However, the major bottleneck lies in the high communication cost of sharing the model with all neighbors in a fully connected network.
To address this issue, Marfoq et al. [158] proposed improving the efficiency of model sharing by selecting a connected subgraph. Another approach is the use of a gossip-based approach, where model parameters or segments of model parameters are randomly shared with peer neighbors [82, 86]. Despite the advantages brought by the decentralized design, the training procedure of these frameworks also follows a preset learning task and lacks sustainable cooperation, resulting in a non-public and low reusability FL platform similar to centralized FL. In fact, our vision for open FL platforms is to extend the FAIR principles [234] for scientific data to the context of machine learning. We believe that all dedicated models in these platforms should adhere to the principles of being Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable.
